<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hong Xiuquan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hong Xiuquan was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> who, after repeatedly failing the civil service examinations, came to believe through visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. He launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> in 1850 and ruled the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> from 1853. He died in 1864 shortly before Qing forces took the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan.jpg" alt="Hong Xiuquan" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ford &amp; West Lith. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Xiuquan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Taiping Rebellion, led by <b>Hong Xiuquan</b>, had held Nanjing since 1853 and much of the rich Yangzi valley; the Nian Rebellion raged north of the Huai River, and Muslim revolts broke out in Yunnan and, from 1862, in the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hong Xiuquan</b> had studied the Bible in Guangzhou with the American Baptist missionary Issachar Jacox Roberts, who refused to baptise him; in 1861 Roberts came to Nanjing, and Hong made him his director of foreign affairs.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In West Africa Usman dan Fodio founded the Sokoto Caliphate (1804–1903), and in China <b>Hong Xiuquan</b>’s Taiping Heavenly Kingdom (1851–1864) fought the Qing in one of the most destructive wars in history.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Qing only about 5 per cent of those who sat the examinations passed, and <b>Hong Xiuquan</b>, after his fourth failure, went on to lead the Taiping Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>Failure could also turn to revolt: <b>Hong Xiuquan</b>, after his fourth failure in the shengyuan examination, went on in the 19th century to lead the Taiping Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hong Xiuquan</b>, a Hakka from Guangdong who repeatedly failed the civil service examinations, came to believe from visions and Christian tracts that he was the younger brother of Jesus.</blockquote><a href="/state/taiping-heavenly-kingdom">Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
